    {
      "page_idx": 27,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "header",
          "index": 0,
          "bbox": [
            0.781,
            0.058,
            0.923,
            0.073
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Contents   xxvii"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "index",
          "index": 1,
          "bbox": [
            0.109,
            0.098,
            0.507,
            0.934
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Attractions Between Nucleosomes Compact the Chromatin Fiber 202  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Summary 203  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "THE EFFECT OF CHROMATIN STRUCTURE ON DNA FUNCTION 203  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Different Regions of the Human Genome Are Packaged Very Differently in Chromatin 204  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Heterochromatin Is Highly Condensed and Restricts Gene Expression 204  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "The Heterochromatic State Can Spread Along a Chromosome and Be Inherited from One Cell Generation to the Next 205  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "The Core Histones Are Covalently Modified at Many Different Sites 206  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Chromatin Acquires Additional Variety Through the Site-specific Insertion of a Small Set of Histone Variants 208  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Covalent Modifications and Histone Variants Can Act in Concert to Control Chromosome Functions 208  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "A Complex of Reader and Writer Proteins Can Spread Specific Chromatin Modifications Along a Chromosome 210  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Barrier DNA-Protein Complexes Block the Spread of Reader-Writer Complexes and Thereby Separate Neighboring Chromatin Domains 212  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Centromeres Have a Special, Inherited Chromatin Structure 213  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Some Forms of Chromatin Can Be Directly Inherited 215  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "The Abnormal Perturbations of Heterochromatin That Arise During Tumor Progression Contribute to Many Cancers 215  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Summary 217  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "THE GLOBAL STRUCTURE OF CHROMOSOMES 217  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Chromosomes Are Folded into Large Loops of Chromatin 217  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Polytene Chromosomes Are Uniquely Useful for Visualizing Chromatin Structures 218  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Chromosome Loops Decondense When the Genes Within Them Are Expressed 220  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Mammalian Interphase Chromosomes Occupy Discrete Territories in the Nucleus, with Their Heterochromatin and Euchromatin Distributed Differently 220  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "A Biochemical Technique Called Hi-C Reveals Details of Chromosome Organization 221  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Chromosomal DNA Is Organized into Loops by Large Protein Rings 223  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Euchromatin and Heterochromatin Separate Spatially in the Nucleus 225  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Mitotic Chromosomes Are Highly Condensed 227  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Summary 228  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "HOW GENOMES EVOLVE 229  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Genome Comparisons Reveal Functional DNA Sequences by Their Conservation Throughout Evolution 230  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Genome Alterations Are Caused by Failures of the Normal Mechanisms for Copying and Maintaining DNA, as Well as by Transposable DNA Elements 231  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "The Genome Sequences of Two Species Differ in Proportion to the Length of Time Since They Have Separately Evolved 232  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Phylogenetic Trees Constructed from a Comparison of DNA Sequences Trace the Relationships of All Organisms 233  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "A Comparison of Human and Mouse Chromosomes Shows How the Structures of Genomes Diverge 234  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "The Size of a Vertebrate Genome Reflects the Relative Rates of DNA Addition and DNA Loss in a Lineage 236  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Multispecies Sequence Comparisons Identify Many Conserved DNA Sequences of Unknown Function 237  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Changes in Previously Conserved Sequences Can Help Decipher Critical Steps in Evolution 238  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Mutations in the DNA Sequences That Control Gene Expression Have Driven Many of the Evolutionary Changes in Vertebrates 239  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Gene Duplication Also Provides an Important Source of Genetic Novelty During Evolution 240  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Duplicated Genes Diverge 240  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "The Evolution of the Globin Gene Family Shows How DNA Duplications Contribute to the Evolution of Organisms 241  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Genes Encoding New Proteins Can Be Created by the Recombination of Exons 242  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Neutral Mutations Often Spread to Become Fixed in a Population, with a Probability That Depends on Population Size 243"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "index",
          "index": 2,
          "bbox": [
            0.527,
            0.098,
            0.924,
            0.247
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "We Can Trace Human History by Analyzing Genomes 244  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "The Sequencing of Hundreds of Thousands of Human Genomes Reveals Much Variation 245  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Most of the Variants Observed in the Human Population Are Common Alleles, with at Most a Weak Effect on Phenotype 246  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Forensic Analyses Exploit Special DNA Sequences with Unusually High Mutation Rates 247  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "An Understanding of Human Variation Is Critical for Improving Medicine 248  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Summary 248  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Problems 249  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "References 251"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.528,
            0.263,
            0.783,
            0.292
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Chapter 5 DNA Replication, Repair, and Recombination"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "index",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.526,
            0.29,
            0.924,
            0.935
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "THE MAINTENANCE OF DNA SEQUENCES 253  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Mutation Rates Are Extremely Low 253  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Low Mutation Rates Are Necessary for Life as We Know It 254  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Summary 255  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "DNA REPLICATION MECHANISMS 255  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Base-pairing Underlies DNA Replication and DNA Repair 255  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "The DNA Replication Fork Is Asymmetrical 256  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "The High Fidelity of DNA Replication Requires Several Proofreading Mechanisms 258  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "DNA Replication in the 5'-to-3' Direction Allows Efficient Error Correction 260  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "A Special Nucleotide-polymerizing Enzyme Synthesizes Short RNA Primer Molecules 260  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Special Proteins Help to Open Up the DNA Double Helix in Front of the Replication Fork 261  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "A Sliding Ring Holds a Moving DNA Polymerase onto the DNA 262  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "The Proteins at a Replication Fork Cooperate to Form a Replication Machine 263  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "DNA Replication Is Fundamentally Similar in Eukaryotes and Bacteria 265  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "A Strand-directed Mismatch Repair System Removes Replication Errors That Remain in the Wake of the Replication Machine 267  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "The Accidental Incorporation of Ribonucleotides During DNA Replication Is Corrected 269  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "DNA Topoisomerases Prevent DNA Tangling During Replication 269  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Summary 272  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "THE INITIATION AND COMPLETION OF DNA REPLICATION IN CHROMOSOMES 272  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "DNA Synthesis Begins at Replication Origins 272  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Bacterial Chromosomes Typically Have a Single Origin of DNA Replication 273  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Eukaryotic Chromosomes Contain Multiple Origins of Replication 273  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "In Eukaryotes, DNA Replication Takes Place During Only One Part of the Cell Cycle 276  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Eukaryotic Origins of Replication Are \"Licensed\" for Replication by the Assembly of an Origin Recognition Complex 276  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Features of the Human Genome That Specify Origins of Replication Remain to Be Fully Understood 277  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Properties of the ORC Ensure That Each Region of the DNA Is Replicated Once and Only Once in Each S Phase 277  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "New Nucleosomes Are Assembled Behind the Replication Fork 279  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Termination of DNA Replication Occurs Through the Ordered Disassembly of the Replication Fork 280  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Telomerase Replicates the Ends of Chromosomes 281  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Telomeres Are Packaged into Specialized Structures That Protect the Ends of Chromosomes 282  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Telomere Length Is Regulated by Cells and Organisms 282  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Summary 284  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "DNA REPAIR 284  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Without DNA Repair, Spontaneous DNA Damage Would Rapidly Change DNA Sequences 286  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "The DNA Double Helix Is Readily Repaired 288  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "DNA Damage Can Be Removed by More Than One Pathway 288"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
